# Button group / segmented control

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Home Assistant uses rows of connected buttons inside some of its cards: the keypad of the alarm panel, the mode buttons on climate cards and the view switch on the calendar. This page explains what they are, what a theme can change, and what to use when you build your own choice.

They are building blocks inside cards. There is no `type:` in YAML that places one on its own dashboard.

This page covers:

- Button group / segmented control
  - `ha-control-button-group`
    - `ha-control-button`
  - `ha-button-toggle-group`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A group is a row (or, for the control button group, optionally a column) of buttons with equal spacing. Each button has an icon or a short text label. One option can look selected.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-control-button-group"><code>ha-control-button-group</code></h3>

A plain container that lays out `ha-control-button` children in a row, or in a column when it is set to `vertical`. It is not a radio group. It has no role, no selection and no events of its own. Spacing is 12px and the default button thickness is 40px, set by `--control-button-group-spacing` and `--control-button-group-thickness`.

<h3 id="child-ha-control-button-group--ha-control-button"><code>ha-control-button</code></h3>

One button in the group, 40px by 40px. It holds an icon or text, and its `label` names it. Disabled buttons use the disabled text colour.

Keyboard focus is shown as a 2px ring in the secondary text colour, set by `--control-button-focus-color`. The button has no selected state of its own.

Theme keys that reach it: `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color` and `disabled-text-color`. Corners use Home Assistant’s own radius, because the Civica theme does not set `ha-border-radius-md`. With the card-mod integration you can make them square, as the [Alarm panel card](alarm-panel-card.html) page shows:

```yaml
card_mod:
  style: |
    ha-control-button { --control-button-border-radius: 0; }
```

<h3 id="child-ha-button-toggle-group"><code>ha-button-toggle-group</code></h3>

A segmented control made of `ha-button` elements, with one option selected. The selected option uses the accent look and the others use the filled look. Icon-only options get a tooltip with their label. When a person picks another option, the group reports the new value straight away. The calendar card uses it, as icon buttons with tooltips, to switch between Month, Day and List (7 days).

The group takes a `size` of `s` or `m`, and variants `brand`, `neutral`, `success`, `warning` or `danger`. The selected option is shown by fill only. The Home Assistant code for the group sets no pressed or checked state, so do not assume that a screen reader announces which option is selected.

Theme keys that reach it: `ha-button-border-radius` (square in Civica), `ha-button-height` (44px in Civica, which may override the small size) and the role fill colours. See [Button](buttons.html#child-ha-button). Because the theme sets the height globally, check the real height in your own dashboard.

You cannot add pressed or radio semantics to either group with a theme or YAML, or change how many options a card offers.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Compact, and familiar to anyone who has used Home Assistant.
- Selection shown by fill alone is often missed, and not visible to people who cannot tell the fills apart.
- Icon-only options rely on a tooltip, which shows only on hover.
- The groups are fixed inside their cards, so you cannot reuse them elsewhere.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

When you choose one option from a short list yourself, use a [radio group](radios.html), or [Tabs](tabs.html) to switch between panels of content. They carry the selected state in the page.

Where you do build a segmented control, offer two to five short options, mark exactly one as selected, and give each a text label. Do not use icon-only options. Do not use a segmented control for a command with a consequence, because choosing an option takes effect straight away.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the selected option must be exposed in the page. The Home Assistant toggle group does not do this in its own code.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: add a cue beyond fill, such as text or a mark.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: measure the selected and unselected edges.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: label the group.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.4.7 Focus Visible.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): control buttons are 40px.
- 3.2.2 On Input: choosing an option acts at once.


